{"subjectId":"sql","id":"create-alter-drop","title":"CREATE, ALTER, DROP & TRUNCATE","group":"Defining & Changing Data","order":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">DDL commands create and reshape database objects. They change the <em>schema</em>, so they are usually run through versioned migration scripts, not by hand.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table-plus\"></i>CREATE TABLE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE projects (\n    project_id  INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n    title       VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    budget      DECIMAL(12, 2) DEFAULT 0,\n    dept_id     INTEGER REFERENCES departments(dept_id),\n    started_on  DATE\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS projects (id INTEGER);   -- no error: already exists, skipped\n\nSELECT name, type, \"notnull\", dflt_value, pk\nFROM pragma_table_info('projects')\nORDER BY cid;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name       | type           | notnull | dflt_value | pk\n-----------+----------------+---------+------------+---\nproject_id | INTEGER        | 0       | NULL       | 1\ntitle      | VARCHAR(100)   | 1       | NULL       | 0\nbudget     | DECIMAL(12, 2) | 0       | 0          | 0\ndept_id    | INTEGER        | 0       | NULL       | 0\nstarted_on | DATE           | 0       | NULL       | 0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>pragma_table_info()</code> is SQLite's way to describe a table. Elsewhere: <code>DESCRIBE t</code> (MySQL), <code>\\d t</code> (psql), <code>sp_help 't'</code> (SQL Server), or the standard <code>information_schema.columns</code> view.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>CREATE TABLE … AS SELECT</h3>\n<p>Creates a table <em>and</em> fills it from a query — handy for snapshots and backups. Constraints and indexes are <strong>not</strong> copied.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE eng_staff AS\nSELECT emp_id, name, salary FROM employees WHERE dept_id = 1;\n\nSELECT * FROM eng_staff ORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>emp_id | name  | salary\n-------+-------+-------\n1      | Asha  | 150000\n2      | Ravi  | 120000\n3      | Meera | 95000\n4      | Karan | 95000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL Server (SELECT … INTO)</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT emp_id, name, salary\nINTO eng_staff\nFROM employees WHERE dept_id = 1;</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-edit\"></i>ALTER TABLE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE projects (project_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL);\n\nALTER TABLE projects ADD COLUMN status TEXT DEFAULT 'planned';\nALTER TABLE projects RENAME COLUMN title TO project_name;\nALTER TABLE projects RENAME TO initiatives;\nINSERT INTO initiatives (project_name) VALUES ('Mobile app');\nALTER TABLE initiatives DROP COLUMN status;\n\nSELECT * FROM initiatives;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>project_id | project_name\n-----------+-------------\n1          | Mobile app</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>SQLite</th><th>MySQL</th><th>PostgreSQL</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Add column</td><td><code>ADD COLUMN c T</code></td><td><code>ADD COLUMN c T</code></td><td><code>ADD COLUMN c T</code></td></tr><tr><td>Rename column</td><td><code>RENAME COLUMN a TO b</code></td><td><code>RENAME COLUMN a TO b</code></td><td><code>RENAME COLUMN a TO b</code></td></tr><tr><td>Change type</td><td>not supported — rebuild the table</td><td><code>MODIFY COLUMN c NEWTYPE</code></td><td><code>ALTER COLUMN c TYPE NEWTYPE</code></td></tr><tr><td>Drop column</td><td><code>DROP COLUMN c</code> (3.35+)</td><td><code>DROP COLUMN c</code></td><td><code>DROP COLUMN c</code></td></tr><tr><td>Add constraint</td><td>not supported — rebuild</td><td><code>ADD CONSTRAINT …</code></td><td><code>ADD CONSTRAINT …</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>MySQL / PostgreSQL</figcaption><pre><code>ALTER TABLE employees MODIFY COLUMN name VARCHAR(150) NOT NULL;   -- MySQL\nALTER TABLE employees ALTER COLUMN name TYPE VARCHAR(150);        -- PostgreSQL\nALTER TABLE employees ADD CONSTRAINT chk_sal CHECK (salary &lt; 1000000);</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trash\"></i>DROP, TRUNCATE, DELETE</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>DELETE FROM t</code></th><th><code>TRUNCATE TABLE t</code></th><th><code>DROP TABLE t</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Removes</td><td>Rows (all, or those matching <code>WHERE</code>)</td><td>All rows</td><td>Rows <strong>and</strong> the table definition</td></tr><tr><td>Category</td><td>DML</td><td>DDL</td><td>DDL</td></tr><tr><td><code>WHERE</code> allowed</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Speed on big tables</td><td>Slow — row by row, fully logged</td><td>Fast — deallocates pages</td><td>Fast</td></tr><tr><td>Fires triggers</td><td>Yes (<code>DELETE</code> triggers)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Resets auto-increment</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (most engines)</td><td>Table is gone</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback</td><td>Yes</td><td>PostgreSQL/SQL Server: yes in a transaction; MySQL/Oracle: no (auto-commit)</td><td>Same as <code>TRUNCATE</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE temp_log (msg TEXT);\nINSERT INTO temp_log VALUES ('a'), ('b');\n\nDELETE FROM temp_log;                 -- SQLite has no TRUNCATE; an unqualified DELETE is optimised the same way\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS rows_left FROM temp_log;\n\nDROP TABLE temp_log;\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS temp_log;        -- no error the second time\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS tables_named_temp_log FROM sqlite_schema WHERE name = 'temp_log';</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>rows_left\n---------\n0\n\ntables_named_temp_log\n---------------------\n0</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>MySQL / PostgreSQL / SQL Server / Oracle</figcaption><pre><code>TRUNCATE TABLE temp_log;</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>DROP</code> and <code>TRUNCATE</code> cannot be undone in MySQL and Oracle (they auto-commit). Take a backup, and never run DDL on production without a reviewed migration.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP?</summary><div><p><code>DELETE</code> removes chosen rows one by one (DML, logged, triggers fire, rollback-able). <code>TRUNCATE</code> empties the whole table quickly by deallocating storage (DDL, no <code>WHERE</code>, no triggers, resets identity). <code>DROP</code> removes the table itself — structure, data, indexes, constraints.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can TRUNCATE be used on a table referenced by a foreign key?</summary><div><p>Generally no — SQL Server and MySQL refuse to truncate a table referenced by a foreign key; PostgreSQL requires <code>TRUNCATE … CASCADE</code> to also empty the referencing tables. <code>DELETE</code> works if no child rows reference the deleted rows.</p></div></details></section>"}